      content:
          '**Primary Diagnosis:** Bilateral severe carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS) with thenar atrophy — likely combined occupational and metabolic etiology.\n\n**EMG/NCS Severity Grading (Expected Findings):**\n- **Sensory NCS:** Absent or severely prolonged median sensory distal latency (>4.5 ms) and reduced/absent SNAP amplitude bilateral. Comparative studies (median vs ulnar to ring finger) will show >0.5 ms difference.\n- **Motor NCS:** Prolonged median motor distal latency (>6.0 ms expected given severity), reduced CMAP amplitude from APB (reflecting axonal loss/thenar atrophy). Right worse than left.\n- **Needle EMG of APB:** Fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves (active denervation), chronic neurogenic changes (large amplitude, long duration, polyphasic MUPs), reduced recruitment — all indicating severe, chronic median neuropathy with axonal loss.\n- **Severity classification:** Stevens/AANEM severe grade — motor and sensory axonal loss with denervation. This is beyond the moderate stage where conservative management alone would suffice.\n\n**Critical Differential Diagnosis:**\n- **Cervical radiculopathy (C6-C7):** Excluded by negative Spurling, normal reflexes, normal non-median innervated muscles, no neck pain. EMG should sample C6-C7 myotomes (pronator teres, FCR, triceps) to confirm.\n- **Thoracic outlet syndrome:** No vascular symptoms, negative Adson, no supraclavicular pathology. But should be considered given bilateral symptoms in a manual laborer.\n- **Diabetic polyneuropathy:** Must check distal lower extremity NCS. If sural SNAP is also reduced, superimposed polyneuropathy is likely contributing. Diabetes also increases CTS risk by 2-3x.\n- **Proximal median neuropathy (pronator syndrome):** No forearm tenderness, no weakness of FPL/FDP to index (anterior interosseous branch). Ruled out clinically.',
