          '**Surgical Planning:**\n- **Carpal tunnel release indicated bilaterally** — severe CTS with axonal loss and thenar atrophy is a clear surgical indication\n- **Open vs endoscopic:** Both have equivalent long-term outcomes. Open release has slightly higher pillar pain and longer return to manual work. Endoscopic has slightly higher risk of incomplete release and nerve injury. For a manual laborer, open release may be preferred for complete visualization and lower revision rate.\n- **Staged approach:** Operate on right (dominant/worse) side first, left side 4-6 weeks later once right hand functional for ADLs.\n\n**Post-Operative Rehabilitation:**\n- Week 1-2: Wound care, edema management, gentle finger ROM. Wrist splint in neutral for comfort (not mandatory).\n- Week 2-4: Progressive wrist ROM, scar mobilization/desensitization, light grip strengthening.\n- Week 4-8: Progressive strengthening, work simulation tasks, power grip and pinch strengthening.\n- Week 8-12: Work hardening program if returning to manual labor.\n\n**Thenar Strengthening Program:**\n- Opponens exercises, thumb abduction against resistance, pinch strengthening with therapy putty\n- Realistic expectation: with chronic denervation and atrophy, full APB recovery is unlikely. Some reinnervation expected over 6-12 months.\n\n**Metabolic Optimization:**\n- Glycemic control optimization — A1c target <7%\n- TSH monitoring — hypothyroidism contributes to CTS\n- Weight management counseling\n\n**Splinting:** Continue nighttime neutral wrist splints bilateral until surgery to minimize nocturnal symptoms.',
