          '**Q: Six months after bilateral carpal tunnel release, her grip strength has improved but she still has significant numbness in her right index finger. What are your next steps?**\n\n**A:** Persistent numbness 6 months post-release requires systematic evaluation:\n1. **Repeat EMG/NCS:** Compare to pre-operative baseline. If median motor latency has improved and CMAP amplitude increased, decompression was successful for the motor component. Persistent sensory loss may reflect **irreversible axonal damage from prolonged compression** or contribution from diabetic polyneuropathy.\n2. **Assess for incomplete release:** If median motor latency is unchanged or worse, consider ultrasound evaluation for incomplete transverse carpal ligament release or post-surgical scar/fibrosis compressing the nerve.\n3. **Evaluate polyneuropathy contribution:** If sural and lower extremity NCS have worsened, the polyneuropathy is progressing and contributing to ongoing sensory loss. Optimize glycemic control.\n4. **Sensory re-education program:** Desensitization and cortical re-mapping techniques (texture discrimination training, Semmes-Weinstein monofilament progression) can improve functional sensory use even without full nerve recovery.\n5. **Adaptive strategies:** Textured grips on tools, visual compensation for diminished protective sensation, skin inspection routine to prevent unnoticed injuries.\n6. **Counsel the patient:** Motor improvement (grip, pinch) is the primary surgical goal and has been achieved. Sensory recovery is slower (up to 18 months) and may be permanently incomplete given the severity and concurrent polyneuropathy.',
